      "content": "Absolutely, the threats you're mitigating by migrating to hardware tokens are significant. Switching away from SMS MFA protects against the following dangers:\n\n1. SIM Squatting: Attackers lease SIM cards from carriers to intercept SMS MFA codes.\n\n2. Phishing Attacks: Unwary users might fall for phishing schemes, mistaking malicious requests for legitimate MFA steps.\n\n3. Man-in-the-Middle Attacks: Where SMS traffic is unencrypted, it can be intercepted en route.\n\n4. Relay Attacks: Compromised devices can relay SMS codes legitimately sent to another device, falsely authenticating the user.\n\n5. High Risk of Roaming Interception: In a law firm traveling internationally, SMS codes can be harder to secure against interception.\n\nHardware MFA keys address these vulnerabilities by using a physical token that only works with the corresponding software at the authenticating server which validates the identity of a user through cryptographic means. They are designed to resist phishing, relay, and man-in-the-middle attacks, and as they're not reliant on SMS, they are more resilient to role-based access control breaches when on the move. They also create a zero-touch, non-operator-centric authentication method, reducing overhead. By implementing hardware security keys, you're significantly reducing these risks.",
